Arcade bar: the SH4 JIT gives the same machine on every computer, whatever it compiled when.

Two things in rec_wasm.cpp (retrom-project/flycast-wasm's SH4 -> WebAssembly JIT) made the game
depend on the host rather than only on the state and the inputs; two machines in lockstep from
the same state drifted apart within a second (flycast/check.mjs finds them):

- Deferred compiles. Upstream compiles a new block synchronously until the frame has used 24 ms
  of wall time, then in the background, running the block meanwhile through the SHIL bridge
  (fly_bridge_execute). The bridge charges the guest other cycles than the compiled block: no
  idle-loop fast-forward, and cycles an op itself adds are dropped. Which blocks were bridged,
  decided by the wall clock and by when the browser's compiles landed, changed the game. Here a
  new block is always compiled on the spot (g_defer_runtime off). flycast_jit_mode(n) brings the
  deferral back for comparisons (check.mjs-style tests show it diverging).
- The import "thunk resolution". The first time a block compiled, the JIT called each of its
  helpers once with dummy arguments: a store-queue flush (which wrote the store queue into RAM
  at 0x0C000000), an 8/16/32-bit write to SH4 address 0, opcode 0 through the interpreter, and a
  DIV1 step that rewrote SR.Q and SR.M. A state loaded before that first compile (a start-up
  state; a player joining) was thereby changed. Our link binds Module._wasm_* straight to the
  module's exports, so the calls are dropped.

- NaNs. WebAssembly leaves the sign and payload of a NaN result to the host CPU (x86 makes
  0xFFC00000 where ARM makes 0x7FC00000): players on an Intel and on an Apple Silicon machine
  would part the first time the game kept the bits of a 0/0. The JIT's FPU arithmetic (fadd,
  fsub, fmul, fdiv, fsqrt, fmac, fsrra, fipr, ftrv) stores any NaN as the SH4's own quiet NaN,
  0x7FBFFFFF (wasm_emit.h), and the interpreter, which runs the double-precision ops, does the
  same through fixNaN/fixNaN64 (sh4_core.h; upstream only in STRICT_MODE). A precaution: no
  NaN reached the RAM in our Virtua Tennis runs (same RAM with and without it), and it costs
  nothing measurable.

The multi-block chain modules, also compiled in the background and adopted when they land, are
kept: check.mjs shows a run where none land and one where they do give the same RAM.

